<H2>Physical Storage in Oracle</H2>
<H3>The Logical Database Structure</H3>
<H3>Tablespace</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>an Oracle database consists of one or more logical storage units =
called=20
  tablespaces=20
  <LI>each tablespace in a database consists of one or more physical =
files=20
  called datafiles; datafiles are standard operating system files; a =
particular=20
  datafile is associated with only one tablespace </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI>tablespaces can be used to control disk space allocation for =
database=20
  data, set quotas for users, allocate database storage across devices =
to=20
  improve performance=20
  <LI>a tablespace is created with the CREATE TABLESPACE command; =
default=20
  storage specifications (extent size details) and the physical data =
file(s) are=20
  specified in this command </LI></UL>
<H3>Segment</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>a segment is the space allocated within a tablespace for a =
particular=20
  logical storage structure=20
  <LI>for example, each table is stored in a segment=20
  <LI>also have index segments, temporary segments and rollback segments =

</LI></UL>
<H3><BR>Extent</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>each segment is made up of one or more extents=20
  <LI>an extent is a group of contiguous blocks </LI></UL>
<H3><BR>Oracle Block</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>smallest unit of storage </LI></UL>
<P><BR><IMG height=3D516=20
src=3D"http://elearning.algonquincollege.com/coursemat/dat2355d/storage.J=
PG"=20
width=3D526>=20
<H3>Types of Tablespaces</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>&nbsp;SYSTEM tablespace - contains the system catalogs, stored =
PL/SQL=20
  programs (includes triggers); this tablespace must be allocated=20
  <LI>USER tablespace - used to store user data=20
  <LI>TEMPORARY tablespace - used for temporary storage - for example, =
for sorts=20
  when SQL is processing ORDER BY, GROUP BY, joins and when an index is =
being=20
  created </LI></UL>
<H3><BR>Specifying the Tablespace</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>both the CREATE TABLE and CREATE INDEX statements include an =
optional=20
  clause in which to specify the tablespace=20
  <LI>if this clause is not coded, the object being created is stored in =
the=20
  default tablespace (a default tablespace is specified when a user =
account is=20
  created)=20
  <LI>in the CREATE TABLE or CREATE INDEX command, use the TABLESPACE =
clause to=20
  specify a tablespace other than the default:=20
</LI></UL>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
CREATE TABLE table-name ...... TABLESPACE tablespace-name;=20
<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
CREATE INDEX index-name.... TABLESPACE tablespace-name;=20
<UL>
  <LI>for the primary key index, use the USING INDEX TABLESPACE clause:=20
</LI></UL>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
PRIMARY KEY (column-list)=20
<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
USING INDEX TABLESPACE tablespace-name <BR>&nbsp;=20
<H3>Oracle Data Block</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>the size of a data block is set when the database is created; =
should be a=20
  multiple of the operating system's page size=20
  <LI>a data block contains:=20
  <LI>header information: block address, type of data contained in the =
block=20
  <LI>table directory: specifies what tables have data in the block=20
  <LI>row directory: information on rows in the block=20
  <LI>free space: to allow for insertions and updates; the amount of =
free space=20
  can be set in the CREATE TABLE, CREATE INDEX statements=20
  <LI>row data: table data (or index data)=20
  <LI>each table row is stored in one or more "row pieces"=20
  <LI>if the row fits in a data block, it is stored as a single row =
piece;=20
  otherwise, stored in multiple row pieces in more than one data block =
that are=20
  chained together </LI></UL>
<H3>ROWIDs</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>a rowid is associated with each row; this is a physical address =
for the=20
  row; for rows that are comprised of more than one row piece, the rowid =
is the=20
  address of the first row piece=20
  <LI>the ROWID has the following format: OOOOOOFFFBBBBBBRRR WHERE:=20
  <LI>OOOOOO is the object number - identifies the database segment=20
  <LI>FFF is the datafile number; file numbers are unique within a =
database=20
  <LI>BBBBBB is the data block number that contains the row; the block =
number is=20
  relative to the datafile=20
  <LI>RRR is the row within the block=20
  <LI>every table has a "pseudocolumn" called ROWID=20
  <LI>this column can not be modified by users but can be displayed with =
a=20
  SELECT statement using the reserved word ROWID </LI></UL>
<H3>Indexes</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>Oracle allows B-tree indexes and bitmap indexes=20
  <LI>the type of index is specified on the CREATE INDEX statement:=20
</LI></UL>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;=20
CREATE [ UNIQUE | BITMAP ] INDEX .....=20
<UL>
  <LI>the index is either stored in the user's default tablespace or in =
the=20
  tablespace specified in the CREATE INDEX statement=20
  <LI>the index does not have to be in the same tablespace as the table =
data=20
  <LI>to improve performance, the index tablespace should be mapped to a =

  different disk drive </LI></UL>
<H3>Index-Organized Tables</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>in a standard B-tree index, the bottom level of the index contains =
rowids=20
  of the corresponding data rows in the data component=20
  <LI>in an index-organized table, the table row data is physically =
stored with=20
  the index data=20
  <LI>provides good access by the primary key </LI></UL>
<H3><BR>Clusters</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>clusters group data from more than one table=20
  <LI>improves access for data that is retrieved together, for example =
in joins=20
  <LI>the cluster key is the column (or columns) that the clustered =
tables have=20
  in common=20
  <LI>this is specified when the cluster is created - CREATE CLUSTER =
statement=20
  <LI>all rows with the same cluster key are stored in the same data =
block=20
  <LI>once the cluster has been created, the tables to be included in =
the=20
  cluster are created; their CREATE TABLE statements will include a =
CLUSTER=20
  clause which tells Oracle that the table to be included in a cluster =
and=20
  specifies the cluster column </LI></UL>
<H3><BR>Cluster Index</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>an index for a cluster must be created (before any data can be =
inserted)=20
  <LI>index is created on the cluster key=20
  <LI>the index can either be a B-tree index or a hash index </LI></UL>
<H3><BR>Creating a Cluster</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>first the cluster must be created:=20
</LI></UL>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;=20
CREATE CLUSTER cluster-name (column data-type);=20
<UL>
  <LI>create the cluster index:=20
</LI></UL>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;=20
CREATE INDEX index-name ON CLUSTER cluster-name=20
<BR>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=20
[TABLESPACE tablespace-name];=20
<UL>
  <LI>then the tables to be included in the cluster are created:=20
</LI></UL>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nb=
sp;&nbsp;=20
CREATE TABLE table-name ..... CLUSTER cluster-name (column);=20
<DIR>&nbsp;</DIR>
<H3>Hash Clusters</H3>
<UL>
  <LI>the rows in a hash cluster are stored together based on their hash =
value=20
  (an index cluster stores data together based on the cluster key)=20
  <LI>Oracle provides an internal hash function that can be used or the =
user can=20
  specify the hash function to be used:=20
  <LI>a unique column that is uniformly distributed over its range of =
values can=20
  be used as the hash value=20
  <LI>any valid SQL expression involving a column or columns can be =
specified as=20
  the hash key </LI></UL><BR>&nbsp; <BR>&nbsp; </BODY></HTML>

